course: dictionary
lesson: 11347
title: Reading a stepped tower
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A ziggurat is a very large ancient tower built in steps, with each level smaller than the one below and a temple on the highest level. You may not know this word, but the sentence around it gives clues. «Over the city rose a ziggurat, a stepped tower of mud brick» tells you the word is a name for a stepped tower.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «Each city had a ziggurat, a huge stepped tower of mud brick with a temple on top for the city's god.» The words huge, stepped and temple on top all show what a ziggurat looks like and what it was for. Try your meaning in the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: Ziggurats were built in ancient Mesopotamia, now mostly Iraq, by peoples such as the Sumerians and Babylonians. The word can also describe any building that rises in flat steps like this, not only the ancient towers.
fact: A ziggurat is a very large ancient tower built in steps.
fact: Each level of a ziggurat is smaller than the one below it, and there was a temple on the highest level.
fact: Ziggurats were built in ancient Mesopotamia, now mostly Iraq, by peoples such as the Sumerians and Babylonians.
fact: «Ziggurat» can also mean any building that rises in flat steps like this.
fact: Some people think the Bible's Tower of Babel may be linked to a real ziggurat in Babylon.
exercise: «Each city had a ziggurat, a huge stepped tower of mud brick.» What is a ziggurat? | *A huge stepped tower | A kind of small boat | A small wooden hut | A river that floods the fields
exercise: Which peoples built ziggurats in ancient Mesopotamia? | Ancient Romans | Vikings | *Sumerians and Babylonians | Pirates
exercise: Find and copy one word from «Over the city rose a ziggurat, a stepped tower of mud brick.» that tells you the tower had steps. | *stepped | rose | tower | city
test: What was the temple on top of a ziggurat for? | To hold school plays | To watch the stars at night | To store fish for the city | *For the city's god
test: «Ziggurat» can also describe which kind of building? | A flat-roofed shed | A bridge over a river | A tent in a field | *A building that rises in flat steps, each level smaller
exam: Some people think the Bible's Tower of Babel may be linked to which real building? | A castle in Scotland | *A ziggurat in Babylon | A lighthouse on the coast | A pyramid in Egypt
